<Base title="About" description="Who I am and what I work on.">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>I'm an applied economist. PhD from Georgia Tech, 2024, with primary field in international trade and secondary fields in geospatial economics, microeconomic theory, and networks. My advisors were Tibor Besedes and Usha Nair-Reichert. The dissertation was on network centrality, spatial heterogeneity, and optimal transport in international trade. That is not a dissertation that ended up cited in policy briefs. The methods turned out to travel, though, and the time since has been about taking the connection useful.</p>

  <p>The path through school was longer than the headline suggests. Undergraduate at UNC Chapel Hill in Economics with Political Science, 2014. A summer at the London School of Economics in 2013 on International Economics and European Economic Integration. A master's at the Barcelona School of Economics in 2014-2015 in Economics and Public Policy, with a thesis on the Pico y Placa driving restrictions in Quito, Ecuador, that was a finalist in the BGSE public-policy thesis competition. Another master's at Indiana University Bloomington in 2017 in international trade, game theory, and financial networks. Then to Georgia Tech for the PhD in Economics (2017-2024) and, in parallel, a second master's in Geographic Information Science and Technology (2020-2022) where the primary research was on the remote detection of economic growth and environmental health using multi- and hyper-spectral remote sensing.</p>

  <p>The GIS work matters because most of my research has a geographic-scale aggregation problem hiding inside it somewhere. The decomposition of a trade-network shock and the decomposition of a place-based-credit program turn out to use the same algebra in different settings. The dissertation's network-centrality apparatus and the GIS work on remote-sensing validation are now both feeding the active research program.</p>

  <h2>What I'm doing right now</h2>

  <p>A working paper on the U.S. New Markets Tax Credit. The headline result: the apparent rural mobilization gap, which has been a persistent talking point in CDFI Fund advocacy for a decade, is almost entirely between-CDE selection rather than a within-CDE rural penalty. Inside any given Community Development Entity, rural projects don't leverage worse than urban ones in a way you can statistically detect. The penalty isn't a market problem, it's an allocation problem. That changes the policy lever: send more rural-targeted dollars through high-leverage urban-experienced CDEs with rural carve-outs, rather than subsidize rural projects more inside intermediaries that don't really do leverage. The paper is heading to SSRN in the next few weeks.</p>

  <p>I'm rolling that methodology forward into the European setting with Katia Antunes, who's about to do fieldwork in Lisbon and Dublin. The Portuguese Sociedades de Garantia Mútua (four mutual-guarantee societies, one of them agriculture-specialized) and the Irish SBCI on-lender structure are clean institutional analogues to the U.S. CDE arrangement. Whether the intermediary-selection mechanism transfers across institutional designs or is a U.S. artifact is the question.</p>

  <p>The bigger arc is the <a href="/program">Penumbra program</a>, a multi-paper investigation of closed-loop observation problems in cross-border trade and finance. The arc runs Paper 0 (theory umbrella) through Paper 5 (effective-distance panel with Dr. Elizaveta Gonchar) and Joint Paper 1 (Ukraine wartime reorganization, also with Liz). Liz is my fiancée and most frequent research collaborator. Her Russian and Ukrainian language access and her CHIPS-era hromada-analog methodology are why the Ukraine work is even possible; without her this would be a much narrower research program.</p>

  <h2>The teaching</h2>

  <p>I taught as Instructor of Record at Georgia Tech (Econ 2106 Microeconomics Spring 2021, Econ 2100 Economics and Policy Problems Summer 2020 and Fall 2020) and as a graduate teaching assistant across the department from Econ 2105 (introductory macro) up through Econ 7023 (PhD Econometrics II). I was one of eight inaugural Graduate Teaching Fellows at the Georgia Tech Center for Teaching and Learning in 2018-19, co-leading the new-TA orientation and running workshops on public speaking, large-lecture engagement, and multimedia assessment. Since July 2022, I've been teaching mathematics and microeconomics for non-traditional and low-income students at Campus / Campuswire (online university partnership with MTI College).</p>

  <p>The teaching keeps me honest about my research. If I can't explain a method clearly enough for a competent undergraduate to use it on their own data, I probably don't understand the method as well as I think I do. That conviction is the spine of <a href="https://ihelfrich.github.io/inference-lab/">Inference Lab</a> (applied causal inference, fifteen chapters), <a href="https://ihelfrich.github.io/macroprep/">Macro Prep</a> (intermediate macro with live FRED dashboards), and <a href="https://github.com/ihelfrich/pictures-of-inference">Pictures of Inference</a> (a visual statistics textbook in draft).</p>

  <p>The tutoring is one-to-one work with graduate students and advanced undergraduates. I take maybe four or five clients at a time. If you're trying to figure out whether your data supports an RDD or whether you need to fall back to a fuzzy design, that's the kind of conversation I'm useful for.</p>

  <h2>Languages and the why-it-matters note</h2>

  <p>I work in English, Spanish, Russian, and Ukrainian. The Russian and Ukrainian were originally learned for personal reasons and family connections, but they're now load-bearing in the research: most of the joint work with Liz on Ukrainian wartime reorganization depends on direct access to Ukrstat releases, NBU communications, KSE Institute reports, and Russian-language satellite-analytic research. A monolingual researcher would be reading those a year late, in translated summary, with detail lost. That gap is the substantive reason the joint work with Liz exists in the form it does.</p>

  <h2>Other facts about me that occasionally come up</h2>

  <p>Third-degree black belt in Sin Moo HapKiDo since 2007, originally Fayetteville, North Carolina. Senator of the Year at the Georgia Tech Graduate Student Government in 2020, after a term as VP for Campus Services where the work included a white paper on graduate-student financial security and outcomes, the elimination of certain institutional fees for graduate students, and the campus-wide work on remote operations during COVID-19. The path through music and audio production (Ableton, Adobe Audition, voice-acting work) shows up occasionally in podcast collaborations and in the visual production of the teaching sites.</p>

  <h2>What I don't do</h2>

  <p>I don't ghostwrite. I don't sign onto papers I haven't substantively contributed to. I don't take consulting engagements whose conclusion is already decided. I'm not on Twitter (the account is dormant; Bluesky is the only social I check). I'm not currently taking big institutional retainer work because I'd rather finish the paper.</p>

  <h2>Where I live</h2>

  <p>Smyrna, Georgia, in metro Atlanta. I work mostly from home, occasionally from the library at Tech, and I travel for conferences and fieldwork. The next trip is NEUDC in November if the submission lands; before that, a visit to Lisbon with Katia. <a href="/now">More on the rolling week-by-week.</a></p>

